#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Wave 34: Little Hands, Just Three, a Fifth Time — the fifth 3-only wave.

Wave 33's honest assessment named this wave the same way waves
29-32 named theirs: the age-3 gap (106 against 160 at age 4, gap
54) "closes only by more waves of this kind, at honest quality,
or not at all." Four consecutive 3-only waves have narrowed it
102 -> 90 -> 78 -> 66 -> 54. This is the fifth: twelve more
records banded 3-3, built to the rules waves 30-33 proved —
short (<= 20 minutes), adult-led, big pieces, one rule per game,
the adult as a player — because the band is honest for the same
reason: these are three-year-old moments (a ribbon wand waved in
a walking parade for the country's birthday, ants counted as
they cross on their own road, one chime note each and only when
pointed at), and age 4, holding 160 records, does not miss them.

Measured before this wave (primary category = categories[0],
records covering age 3): holidays and team-community were the
floor at 8 each; seasons / stem / icebreakers / outdoor /
music-rhythm 9 each; crafts 10, pe-movement 10, rainy-day 12,
quiet-focus 13. Rainy-day (33) is still the thinnest primary
category on the whole shelf, then music-rhythm (34) and seasons
(36). So the pack repeats the proven shape exactly: one record
for every one of the 11 categories — the floor rises 8 -> 9
everywhere — plus a second rainy-day record. Result, asserted
in the wave log: age 3 106 -> 118, age 4 unchanged at 160, the
headline gap 54 -> 42, narrowed for the fifth wave running.
Tag delta: July 4th 5 -> 6 (the last holiday tag on the floor
of 5 rises), fall 51 -> 52.

Near-duplicate screen: token Jaccard >= 0.55 against every
existing name+tagline is asserted below and fails the run.
Closest deliberate neighbours, kept on purpose:
- big-ribbon-wand-parade-walk vs star-spangled-shoebox-parade
  (4-12, decorated shoeboxes carried in a marching parade):
  no boxes, no decorating contest, no marching steps to learn —
  a wand in each hand, a walking loop, and the waving IS the
  parade; the skill is two hands waving at different speeds.
- one-tree-autumn-color-count vs one-branch-bud-count (wave 31,
  spring buds counted by fingertip on one low branch): the same
  one-tree patience in the opposite season — nothing is touched
  at all here; colours are counted by pointing, and a colour
  counts when two counters agree they can see it.
- umbrella-drip-count-listen vs rain-drums-on-pots (wave 31,
  the rain PLAYED on upturned pots by the kids' own hands):
  here the kids make no sound at all — the adult makes the rain,
  it falls off a real umbrella's edge, and the group counts the
  drips by ear. Producing weather vs listening to weather.
- big-cotton-cloud-blanket-drift vs blanket-ball-bounce-together
  (a ball kept bouncing on a held blanket, team-community): the
  ball there is one and bouncy and the game is keeping it UP;
  the puffs here are many, nearly weightless, and the game is
  keeping them DRIFTING — the blanket moves in slow waves, and
  a puff that lands is weather that has settled, not a failure.
- big-float-sink-basin-sort vs sink-or-float-lab (older kids,
  predictions and reasons): no predictions are asked at three —
  the object is held over the water, let go, and the basin
  itself gives the report, which the group says in two words.
- big-picnic-blanket-set-together vs long-banner-carry-together
  (wave 31, a banner carried held along its edge): the blanket
  here is carried the same crew way, but carrying is only the
  first act — the teamwork is SETTING the picnic: every plate
  and cup placed by a different pair of hands, in a row the
  whole crew can inspect. And the picnic is pretend, said out
  loud: no food, so nobody's allergy is ever on the blanket.
- big-feelings-card-show-and-say vs feelings-pumpkin-faces
  (crafts, feelings drawn onto pumpkins in October): nothing is
  made here — a big card is chosen, the feeling is named, the
  chooser's own face tries it, and the circle tries it back.
  The card is a mirror with a handle, not a worksheet.
- big-ant-trail-count-watch vs after-rain-worm-count (wave 32,
  residents on a path counted with eyes, touched by nobody):
  worms are met one at a time after rain; the ants are a whole
  working road, always moving in both directions, and the count
  is of crossings — how many pass one leaf in the count of ten.
- big-chime-note-when-pointed vs big-pot-lid-cymbal-band (wave
  33, unpitched crashes cued by the conductor's hands): the lids
  crash TOGETHER on the up-cue; the chimes are pitched, one bar
  per musician, struck ONCE and only when the pointer lands on
  YOU — a scale played by eight separate decisions to wait.
- big-handprint-leaf-tree vs big-finger-paint-sky-sheet (wave
  33, fingertips dabbing one shared sky): fingers there, palms
  here — one print per painter, pressed once, and the print is
  NAMED as that painter's leaf on the group's tree. The sky was
  a surface; the tree is a roster.
- big-feather-blow-float vs giant-bubble-chase (outdoor, older
  kids chasing and popping bubbles): nothing is chased and
  nothing is popped — one big feather, blown at by the circle in
  turn, kept floating by breath alone, and caught by nobody
  until the adult ends the flight by catching it themselves.
- big-button-slot-drop-box vs big-lid-match-basket (wave 30,
  lids matched to their jars by fit): no matching here — the
  button fits the slot or it does not, the drop is the whole
  act, and the box's rattle when shaken afterwards is the count
  of everything the workers put in, taken together.

Safety notes are practical only — no medical claims anywhere.

Run once: dedupes by id, writes data/activities.json + collections.json.
Run scripts/build.py afterwards.
"""
